{:edb/db-before-t 2167 :edb/db-after-t 2168 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535480 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:57:46.219Z" 13194139535480 true] [17592186197978 1100 "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda (x) = (x+4)\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$. Find the moment of the rod about the origin.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4167/Source/Images/ac2d4ddc8a114f8f9399775fec32db1a.png)" 13194139535480 false] [17592186197978 1100 "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda (x) = (x+4)\\,\\textrm{kg/m}.$ Find the moment of the rod about the origin.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040800/assets/7f88ca365b75320795e9d38170a8586399d759696638a4bd78c73ca6c6f44b1a.png)" 13194139535480 true] [17592186197978 1145 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x+4, \\qquad a=0, \\qquad b=3\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{O} &= ∫_{0}^{3}x \\cdot (x + 4)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}x^{2} + 4xdx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + 2x^{2}]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3^{3}}{3} + 2 \\cdot 3^{2}) - 0 \\\\\n&= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required moment is $27\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot \\textrm{m}$." 13194139535480 false] [17592186197978 1145 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by \n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x+4, \\qquad \na=0, \\qquad \nb=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\cdot (x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}+4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+2\\cdot {3}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $27\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot \\textrm{m}.$" 13194139535480 true] [17592186197979 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040800/assets/461c4cb760541ea435620803689e193d13c9cb4706a59b031d37da6ffd49ab9f.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x) = x^3.$ Given that the rod's mass is $4$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139535480 true] [17592186197979 1100 "The thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x) = x^3$. Given that the rod's mass is $4$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4167/Source/Images/8c63fd7d44dfaf371d96c176253bfc52.png)" 13194139535480 false] [17592186197979 1145 "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhose mass density function is $\\lambda(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\int_a^b \\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\int_a^b \\, x\\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x^3, \\qquad a=0, \\qquad b=2\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{O} &= ∫_{0}^{2}x \\cdot x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{5}x^{5}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(32 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{32}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}= \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{32}{5}\\right)}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nNote that this result makes intuitive sense. Since $\\lambda$ is an increasing function of $x$, we would expect the center of mass to be located closer to the right endpoint and further from the left endpoint." 13194139535480 false] [17592186197979 1145 "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\int_a^b \\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\int_a^b \\, x\\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x^3, \\qquad \na=0, \\qquad \nb=2.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x}= \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{32}{5}\\right)}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that this result makes intuitive sense. Since $\\lambda$ is an increasing function of $x,$ we would expect the center of mass to be located closer to the right endpoint and further from the left endpoint." 13194139535480 true] [17592186197980 1100 "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda(x) =2x\\, \\textrm{kg/m}$, where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4167/Source/Images/ab9b1a56295dd675dc0079231bebfe76.png)" 13194139535480 false] [17592186197980 1100 "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda(x) =2x\\, \\textrm{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040800/assets/8c8386ff979b46cd994f1ace60f681a6590856e6960e7d74c71a29bb3f0f2d1a.png)" 13194139535480 true] [17592186197980 1145 "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b,$ if $\\lambda(x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm = \\int_a^b \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x) = 2x, \\qquad \na=1, \\qquad \nb=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\textrm{kg}.$" 13194139535480 true] [17592186197980 1145 "Given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nif $\\lambda(x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\int_a^b \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x) = 2x, \\qquad a=1, \\qquad b=3\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ∫_{1}^{3}2xdx \\\\\n&= [x^{2}]_{1}^{3} \\\\\n&= 3^{2} - 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\textrm{kg}$." 13194139535480 false] [17592186198335 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351407 13194139535480 true] [17592186198335 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351414 13194139535480 true] [17592186198335 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351421 13194139535480 true] [17592186198335 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351428 13194139535480 true] [17592186198336 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351344 13194139535480 true] [17592186198336 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351358 13194139535480 true] [17592186198336 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351365 13194139535480 true] [17592186198336 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351372 13194139535480 true] [17592186198337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351351 13194139535480 true] [17592186198337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351379 13194139535480 true] [17592186198337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351386 13194139535480 true] [17592186198337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351393 13194139535480 true] [17592186198337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351400 13194139535480 true] [17592186198565 1017 #edb/ref 17592186194291 13194139535480 false] [17592186198565 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351338 13194139535480 true] [17592186198565 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535480 true] [17592186198565 1100 "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)=4{x}^{2},$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 2,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139535480 true] [17592186198565 1100 "A thin rod with mass density function $λ(x) = 4x^{2}$, measured in $kg/m$, is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1 \\le x \\le 2$, where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139535480 false] [17592186198565 1145 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=4{x}^{2},\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot 4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-{1}^{4} \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $15\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139535480 true] [17592186198567 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535480 true] [17592186198567 1100 "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2$ whose mass density function is $λ(x) = 3x$. Given that the rod's mass is $6$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139535480 false] [17592186198567 1100 "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 2$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=3x.$ Given that the rod's mass is $6$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139535480 true] [17592186198567 1145 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3x,\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{8}{6}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351339 13194139535480 true] [17592186351338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351340 13194139535480 true] [17592186351338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351341 13194139535480 true] [17592186351338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351342 13194139535480 true] [17592186351338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351343 13194139535480 true] [17592186351338 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535480 true] [17592186351338 1041 "selection" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351338 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351343 13194139535480 true] [17592186351338 1110 #uuid "5f7a8dc4-cc36-515c-899d-27c490389e7f" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351339 1034 "16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351339 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351339 1111 #uuid "1f64918a-aa67-57da-a49d-a064737ee08a" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351340 1034 "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351340 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351340 1111 #uuid "d4cf2436-01bf-524c-ae30-d9a646b4192f" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351341 1034 "10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351341 1111 #uuid "db508f40-59c5-5289-b494-b3a62dcc10bb" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351342 1034 "14\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351342 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351342 1111 #uuid "bc1a85e7-6409-5c20-aac7-8e770bf6d76c" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351343 1034 "15\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351343 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351343 1111 #uuid "ac143dd4-4405-5688-9d97-ff459d4582f6" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351344 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351345 13194139535480 true] [17592186351344 1089 "q-162981" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351344 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535480 true] [17592186351344 1100 "Consider a thin rod along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 4$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass of the rod." 13194139535480 true] [17592186351344 1113 #uuid "0d991181-2738-52fe-8913-289426ec5b40" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351344 1145 "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,a=0,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {4}^{5/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{64}{5})}{(\\frac{16}{3})}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351346 13194139535480 true] [17592186351345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351347 13194139535480 true] [17592186351345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351348 13194139535480 true] [17592186351345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351349 13194139535480 true] [17592186351345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351350 13194139535480 true] [17592186351345 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535480 true] [17592186351345 1041 "selection" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351345 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351350 13194139535480 true] [17592186351345 1110 #uuid "bc8b43c7-5669-51d0-926d-c16a7838488d" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351346 1034 "\\frac{16}{3}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351346 1111 #uuid "aaba3ad6-c108-5ed6-9212-15d5c0cd34cb" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351347 1034 "\\frac{64}{5}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351347 1111 #uuid "4273ca3d-0355-50bc-9870-61cf6d377c14" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351348 1034 "\\frac{32}{3}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351348 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351348 1111 #uuid "85e5472e-4f59-57b0-8852-7a8d2e6e0843" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351349 1034 "\\frac{16}{5}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351349 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351349 1111 #uuid "ea3a311d-5a1e-5425-9566-352f6febc698" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351350 1034 "\\frac{12}{5}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351350 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351350 1111 #uuid "e2efb028-c4d5-5671-9bc1-3c07e556802d" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351351 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351352 13194139535480 true] [17592186351351 1089 "q-163013" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351351 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535480 true] [17592186351351 1100 "The mass density function $\\lambda ,$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ of a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 2$ is given by $\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}+1,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351351 1113 #uuid "dc411752-e7d5-5473-a92e-af7ce4235eb3" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351351 1145 "Given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by  \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}+1,\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =({2}^{3}+2)-({1}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\text{kg}.$" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351352 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351353 13194139535480 true] [17592186351352 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351354 13194139535480 true] [17592186351352 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351355 13194139535480 true] [17592186351352 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351356 13194139535480 true] [17592186351352 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351357 13194139535480 true] [17592186351352 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535480 true] [17592186351352 1041 "selection" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351352 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351354 13194139535480 true] [17592186351352 1110 #uuid "f1775140-24bb-5fe2-9572-f36ce5269808" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351353 1034 "\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{kg}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351353 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351353 1111 #uuid "80fc6ef4-37f7-5889-8cd3-307d54f097fa" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351354 1034 "8\\,\\text{kg}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351354 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351354 1111 #uuid "c292c459-aa87-5c26-aa31-2687886ea3ed" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351355 1034 "\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{kg}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351355 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351355 1111 #uuid "10d5a9a1-0f0f-5952-bdc3-14690dd35c74" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351356 1034 "12\\,\\text{kg}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351356 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351356 1111 #uuid "a1df0822-126e-5b10-a1db-21188dc8bec1" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351357 1034 "10\\,\\text{kg}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351357 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351357 1111 #uuid "463abff5-66d0-55c3-9a67-478ade03c844" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351358 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351359 13194139535480 true] [17592186351358 1089 "q-163016" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351358 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535480 true] [17592186351358 1100 "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le e$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Given that the rod's mass is $1$ unit, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351358 1113 #uuid "db4151bb-178b-55fb-96c6-b5798c6d3993" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351358 1145 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,a=1,\\,b=e.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{e-1}{1}=e-1.\n$$" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351359 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351360 13194139535480 true] [17592186351359 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351361 13194139535480 true] [17592186351359 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351362 13194139535480 true] [17592186351359 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351363 13194139535480 true] [17592186351359 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351364 13194139535480 true] [17592186351359 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535480 true] [17592186351359 1041 "selection" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351359 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351363 13194139535480 true] [17592186351359 1110 #uuid "aff35934-5480-5c7f-9ed7-d1aec7bc49e7" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351360 1034 "\\frac{2}{e-1}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351360 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351360 1111 #uuid "04eba856-0799-5bf4-9634-5e476b6469d4" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351361 1034 "2e-1" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351361 1111 #uuid "295404d0-cf33-5d75-82fe-eceede4f9284" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351362 1034 "e" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351362 1111 #uuid "ddc60c7f-fa54-5aaa-b1ab-59703a193cb8" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351363 1034 "e-1" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351363 1111 #uuid "23596538-9f89-5745-9b75-6db8e3995fde" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351364 1034 "\\frac{1}{e-1}" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535480 true] [17592186351364 1111 #uuid "73d2ccaf-a34c-5bb5-b9b8-f8fa4ca3594a" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351365 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351366 13194139535480 true] [17592186351365 1089 "q-163019" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351365 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535480 true] [17592186351365 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040800/assets/887bc91e2565fa24630af4cb74b28723783e31f4769495f0a28a622d73d176de.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{5}{6}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351365 1113 #uuid "fc0cd8c9-d9c9-59c8-af1b-b823ec38fb53" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351365 1145 "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{7}{12})}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351366 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351367 13194139535480 true] [17592186351366 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351368 13194139535480 true] [17592186351366 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351369 13194139535480 true] [17592186351366 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351370 13194139535480 true] [17592186351366 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351371 13194139535480 true] [17592186351366 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535480 true] [17592186351366 1041 "selection" 13194139535480 true] [17592186351366 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351371 13194139535480 true] [17592186351366 1110 #u